{
  "image_name": "5a13aff0406da28d328cac16025d41a2.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the PINK1–Parkin pathway that marks damaged mitochondria for autophagic removal, or mitophagy. A mitochondrion is shown with the outer and inner membranes and the TOM/TIM23 import machinery. Under normal polarized conditions, PINK1 appears to be imported through these translocases into the mitochondrion, where it is processed by MPP and PARL and then sent to the proteasome for degradation. This keeps PINK1 levels low on healthy mitochondria.\n\nWhen the membrane potential is lost, indicated by the disrupted proton gradient, PINK1 is no longer fully imported and instead accumulates on the outer mitochondrial membrane. There it recruits Parkin from the cytosol. Parkin then ubiquitinates outer-membrane proteins, including examples such as VDAC1 and MIRO1. These ubiquitin tags help attract the autophagy machinery, shown by LC3-II and the ATG5–ATG12–ATG16L complex on a growing phagophore.\n\nThe diagram also suggests membrane contributions from cardiolipin to the engulfing structure. Overall, the image contrasts healthy mitochondria, where PINK1 is continuously cleaved and degraded, with damaged mitochondria, where stabilized PINK1 activates Parkin-dependent ubiquitination and promotes enclosure by a phagophore for mitochondrial clearance.",
